// Package filenamenormalizer.v1 is the File Normalizer inspection API.
//
// # What this API is for
//
// Inspection, validation and preview. It is deliberately read-only with
// respect to the operational ledger and the filesystem.
//
// RabbitMQ remains the only way work is distributed, and the ledger remains
// the only record of it. No RPC here enqueues a job, claims a file, reserves a
// destination, or writes a ledger row, so there is no second processing path
// that could bypass the durability and recovery guarantees the queue-based one
// provides. A preview tells you what a name WOULD become; it is not a
// reservation, and the name it reports can still be taken by a real submission
// a moment later.
//
// # Privacy
//
// Document identities -- names, paths, fingerprints -- are restricted. The
// default job view carries none of them: it reports an opaque job id, a state,
// a category, counters and timestamps. A caller that genuinely needs the names
// must ask for them explicitly with include_restricted_detail, and the server
// records that disclosure in its own log. PreviewName is different in kind:
// the caller supplies the name, so the response reveals nothing the caller did
// not already have.
//
// # Access boundary
//
// The server binds to the application network only and is not published to the
// host by default. There is no authentication here, which is exactly why it
// must not be exposed: anyone who can reach the port can read restricted job
